PRIOR ART THE SCOUT DID NOT FIND
  [PAPER]  V.M. Weaver, D. Terpstra, S. Moore, "Non-Determinism and Overcount on
           Modern Hardware Performance Counter Implementations", IEEE ISPASS 2013.
           Eleven x86_64 implementations, self-written asm validation, asks exactly
           "are retired-instruction counts deterministic?" Answer: no, and here is
           the per-uarch overcount and the iret mechanism.
           https://web.eece.maine.edu/~vweaver/projects/deterministic/
  [PAPER]  V.M. Weaver, S. McKee, "Can Hardware Performance Counters be Trusted?",
           IISWC 2008 — the candidate's own target venue.
  [REPO]   rr (Mozilla). Rejected INST_RETIRED for replay precisely because it is
           not exact, and uses retired conditional branches instead.
           https://arxiv.org/pdf/1705.05937
  [VENDOR] Linux /sys/devices/cpu/freeze_on_smi + `perf stat --smi-cost`, mainline
           since 2017. Present and =1 on this box.
  [PRODUCT]Cachegrind/Callgrind, iai-callgrind, rustc-perf, CodSpeed: instruction
           count AS the noise-free benchmarking metric, explicitly to escape
           wall-clock noise in virtualized CI. Commercially shipped.
  [PAPER]  B. Delgado, K. Karavanic, "Performance Implications of System Management
           Mode", IISWC 2013 — SMM perturbation characterisation, target venue.
  [PAPER]  Puschner et al., single-path / WCET-oriented programming: "the WCET of
           the single path [is] obtained by executing the code and logging the
           duration" — a fixed instruction stream on bare metal as a measurement
           methodology, since ~2003.
  [TEXT]   Iron law: Time = Instructions x CPI x cycle time. Decomposing a speedup
           into instruction-count ratio and IPC ratio is the DEFAULT methodology at
           IISWC/CGO/PACT, not a contribution to it.
